// knip configuration for `web` (PRD #103 M4). This is the `dead code` gate slot's
// npm half; the Go half is `scripts/deadcode-gate.sh`. Run it as `task deadcode:web`.
//
// JSONC, NOT JSON, DELIBERATELY. knip resolves `knip.jsonc` natively (verified
// against 6.31.0's own `dist/constants.js`), and every suppression below needs a
// REASON next to it -- an `ignore` entry with no stated reason is indistinguishable
// from a mistake, and this repo has paid for suppressions nobody could evaluate.
//
// EVERY FLAG LIVES HERE, SO THE npm SCRIPT IS A BARE `knip`. Two reasons. The
// Taskfile header requires npm targets to delegate to the package.json script, and a
// script carrying flags is one edit away from silently dropping them; and
// `web/package.json` is a documented three-way contention point across PRD #103's
// milestones, so the smallest possible line there is the one least likely to
// conflict.
//
// 🔴 `knip --fix` MODIFIES FILES IN THE REPO (it deletes `export` keywords and can
// delete whole files with --allow-remove-files). It must never appear in a gate
// target, a package.json script, or CI. Nothing in the gate may be a fixing variant.
//
// 🔴 AND THE WEAKER SIBLING HAS NO CI ANSWER, exactly as `.golangci.yml` and both
// `.oxlintrc.json` files record for themselves: a branch author can NEUTER this
// gate from this same file — widen `ignore`, drop a rule to `warn`, or add an
// `ignoreDependencies` entry — and the pipeline stays green, because knip
// genuinely found nothing gating. CODEOWNERS on the seven gate-config files
// (this one, agent/knip.jsonc, .golangci.yml, both .oxlintrc.json, and both
// .deadcode-baseline) is the only structural fix. THERE IS NO CODEOWNERS FILE
// ANYWHERE IN THIS TREE TODAY, so the residual is open for all seven; it is
// recorded in the MR rather than closed here.
{
  // ---- SUPPRESSIONS. Three day-one findings, each verified against the tree. ----

  // `public/theme-preinit.js` is loaded by `web/index.html` as
  // `<script src="/theme-preinit.js">`. Vite copies `public/` verbatim and the
  // reference is a URL rather than an import, so knip cannot resolve it and reports
  // the file as unused. It is an EXTERNAL file specifically so nginx's CSP can stay
  // `script-src 'self'` with no `'unsafe-inline'` (PRD #21, and `web/nginx.conf`'s
  // own comment says so). Acting on the finding would break pre-paint theming in
  // every nginx-served build AND the CSP rationale, in one edit.
  "ignore": ["public/**"],

  // `scripts/gen-favicon-png.mjs` is a hand-run generator (its own header says
  // `node web/scripts/gen-favicon-png.mjs`), so it is an ENTRY POINT that happens to
  // be invoked by a human rather than by a build. Declared as `entry` and not
  // `ignore` on purpose: an entry keeps its imports and its own dependencies in
  // scope, where an ignore would silently drop everything it reaches.
  //
  // Note `scripts/check-docs.mjs` needs no such line: knip's package.json-scripts
  // plugin reaches it through the `check-docs` npm script. The discriminator is
  // "reachable from a package.json script", not "is a script".
  //
  // 🔴 THIS LIST DOES NOT REPLACE THE VITE/VITEST ENTRIES. knip's plugins contribute
  // their own (`index.html` -> `src/main.tsx`, `vite.config.ts`, the test files), and
  // those are additive to this. Verified by measurement, not by assumption: with this
  // key present the report is unchanged except for the one file it removes.
  "entry": ["scripts/gen-favicon-png.mjs"],

  // ---- SEVERITY STAGING (PRD #103 Decision 4, Success Criterion 2). ------------
  //
  // ALL SEVENTEEN RULE NAMES ARE LISTED, including the ones that would land on
  // knip's own default. A rule left out is a severity decided by a default the
  // reader cannot see -- and the failure mode here is silent: a MISTYPED rule name
  // prints `WARNING: Ignored unknown issue type "..."` among the findings and does
  // NOT change the exit code. `--treat-config-hints-as-errors` does not cover it
  // either. The names below were taken from `node_modules/knip/schema.json`
  // (`.properties.rules.properties`), not from memory.
  //
  // 🔴 HISTORY, KEPT BECAUSE IT EXPLAINS WHY `--max-issues` IS NOT IN THIS CONFIG.
  // The export/type rules below shipped as `warn` (PRD #103 M4): reported in full,
  // gating nothing. `--max-issues` could NOT turn that into a soft gate -- measured
  // twice independently, with every rule at `warn`, `--max-issues 0` exits 0 with
  // all findings printed, because --max-issues counts ERROR-severity issues only.
  // The debt was burned down and the tier PROMOTED to `error` (issue #596, the
  // `web` half of #206), so those rules now gate at zero and no regression budget
  // is wanted.
  "rules": {
    // ERROR: zero findings today, so these gate at zero from day one.
    "files": "error",
    "dependencies": "error",
    "devDependencies": "error",
    "optionalPeerDependencies": "error",
    "unlisted": "error",
    "binaries": "error",
    "unresolved": "error",
    "duplicates": "error",
    "catalog": "error",
    "catalogReferences": "error",

    // OFF, and this is the one entry that is a scope decision rather than a debt
    // decision. `cycles` (circular imports) is not part of knip's default report and
    // enabling it is a separate call with its own burn-down; M4 is dead code, not
    // module-graph hygiene. Explicit rather than omitted so nobody reads its absence
    // as an oversight.
    "cycles": "off",

    // ERROR: promoted from `warn` once the debt was burned down (issue #596, the
    // `web` half of #206). The 8 unused value exports were removed/de-exported at
    // the source; the 18 unused exported types are DTO/wire and parsed-shape
    // contracts kept exported for readability and covered by
    // `ignoreExportsUsedInFile` below, not by a blanket suppression. These now gate
    // at zero: a NEW unused export/type reddens `task deadcode:web`.
    "exports": "error",
    "types": "error",
    "nsExports": "error",
    "nsTypes": "error",
    "enumMembers": "error",
    "namespaceMembers": "error"
  },

  // 🔴 KEEPS DTO/CONTRACT TYPES EXPORTED WITHOUT FLAGGING THEM (issue #596). Some
  // exported types are constructed by exactly one file yet exported deliberately, so
  // a reader finds the wire/response shape at the barrel rather than having to open
  // the one call site: the ~13 `src/lib/apiTypes.ts` request/response DTOs
  // (PrivilegeTokenReport, ForgeConfig, CliTokenMint, ScheduleStatus, RunNowResponse,
  // AdminUsageUser, MyRateLimitsResponse, AdminRateLimits, ChatListResponse, ...) plus
  // the parsed-shape types `ReleaseGroup` (changelog.ts), `QuestionOption`
  // (runQuestion.ts) and `ModelTotal` (runUsage.ts). Scoped to `interface`/`type`
  // ONLY -- value exports (function/const/variable) are NOT covered, so a helper that
  // is used only in its own file still reddens and must drop its `export` (that is how
  // the 8 value findings were fixed, not by this key). This is NOT a blanket mute: a
  // type used in NO file at all is still reported, so `types`/`nsTypes` keep gating a
  // genuinely-dead type -- calibrated by the probe recorded in this MR.
  "ignoreExportsUsedInFile": {
    "interface": true,
    "type": true
  },

  // 🔴 OFF BY DEFAULT, AND THAT DEFAULT IS A REAL HOLE HERE. Without this, an unused
  // export in an ENTRY file is never reported at all -- and web's entry set includes
  // `src/main.tsx`, the vite config, and the docs/mock loaders. Turning it on makes
  // the export/type tier honest about the size of the debt instead of hiding part of it.
  "includeEntryExports": true,

  // 🔴 WITHOUT THESE TWO, A CONFIG THAT HAS STOPPED MATCHING DEGRADES SILENTLY TO
  // "no findings". If the `entry` glob above stops resolving, or a `tags` hint goes
  // stale, knip prints a hint and still exits 0 -- a green gate over a config that
  // is no longer doing anything, which is this repo's signature failure shape.
  "treatConfigHintsAsErrors": true,
  "treatTagHintsAsErrors": true
}
